## Heads Up — There May Be a Second Wall

Once the image pulls successfully, `run.sh` inside the image will download the actual K3s binary from **GitHub releases** (`github.com` and `objects.githubusercontent.com`). Those may **also** be TLS-intercepted. If you hit this, the error will be clear: `k3s.service` will fail with a curl/download error.

As a pre-emptive fix, also trust whatever CA intercepts GitHub:

```bash
# Check if GitHub is also intercepted
for host in github.com objects.githubusercontent.com api.github.com; do
  echo -n "=== $host → "
  timeout 8 openssl s_client -connect ${host}:443 -servername ${host} </dev/null 2>&1 \
    | grep -E "verify return|verify error" | head -1
done
```

If any show `verify error`, grab their chains the same way and add to `/etc/pki/trust/anchors/` before `update-ca-certificates`.

## The Real Fix for 5,000 Sites

This diagnostic reveals the **actual** architectural requirement for your rollout:

1. **Get the corporate root CA(s) from your security team.** You shouldn't be extracting CAs from TLS handshakes in production. Request:
   - The corporate root CA certificate
   - Any intermediate CAs used by the proxy (Zscaler, Netskope, etc. typically have 1–2 intermediates)
   - A list of which domains are intercepted vs. bypassed (for planning)

2. **Bake the CA bundle into the Elemental OS image** at build time:
   ```yaml
   # In your Elemental MachineRegistration / cloud-init
   stages:
     initramfs:
       - name: "Install corporate CAs"
         files:
           - path: /etc/pki/trust/anchors/corp-root-ca.crt
             content: |
               -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----
               MIID... (your corp root CA)
               -----END CERTIFICATE-----
             permissions: 0644
         commands:
           - update-ca-certificates
   ```

3. **Better still — deploy a regional Harbor mirror** that's INSIDE your network and NOT subject to TLS interception. Point all 5,000 sites to `harbor.corp.advancestores.com` instead of `docker.io`. Then the TLS interception issue is architecturally impossible because the traffic never leaves your network.

This is now the third time in this thread the registry mirror has come up as the permanent solution. With your scale + your network policy, it's not optional — it's the only sane way to do this.

